    theory: `<p>The driver model compares the reference (cycle) speed with the actual speed and outputs a single command $u\\in[-1,1]$: positive values are applied as accelerator, negative as brake. A PI law $u = K_pe + K_i\\int e\\,dt$ is used with <b>anti-windup</b> (integration stops while the output saturates) and a short <b>preview</b> (the driver looks 0.6 s ahead) plus a feed-forward on the reference acceleration. Too-low gains give lag; too-high gains give pedal oscillation and wasted energy. Default gains are tuned for the reference vehicle.</p>`,
    eqs: [['e = v_{ref} - v,\\quad u = K_pe + K_i\\int e\\,dt + k_{ff}\\dot v_{ref}', ''], ['\\alpha_{acc} = \\max(u,0),\\quad \\alpha_{brk} = \\max(-u,0)', '']],
