    viva: [['Why can recovered energy never equal the kinetic energy?', 'Road loads, drivetrain conversion losses, battery losses, charge-acceptance limits and low-speed fade all reduce it; friction supplies the rest.'], ['What limits regeneration when the battery is nearly full?', 'The BMS charge-power limit tends to zero near 100 % SOC, so friction brakes must take over.'], 'In which quadrant does the motor operate during forward regenerative braking?'],
    mcq: [{ q: 'During regeneration the battery current is', o: ['Positive (discharging)', 'Negative (charging)', 'Zero', 'AC'], a: 1 }, { q: 'Regeneration is reduced to zero at very low speed because', o: ['Battery is full', 'Back-EMF and recoverable power are too small', 'Friction brakes are off', 'Motor overheats'], a: 1 }],
